        {
          "store": "...",
          "product": "...",
          "common_name": "...",
          "sub_category": "..."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

The exact structure may be improved if needed.

Keep the input compact enough for the configured Ollama context window.

Avoid sending redundant purchase history records.

=======================================================
BATCHING
========

Support batching so a large Category Manager does not exceed Ollama's context window.

Use a conservative batch size.

A value around:

```
10-25 unresolved Sub-Categories per request
```

is reasonable, but choose an implementation appropriate for the existing context-window handling.

Every batch must still receive enough existing taxonomy information to classify its targets consistently.

=======================================================
REQUIRED OLLAMA OUTPUT
======================

Require JSON only.

Use a structure such as:

```
{
  "rows": [
    {
      "excel_row": 17,
      "sub_category": "Fresh Fruit",
      "category": "Food"
    },
    {
      "excel_row": 29,
      "sub_category": "Motor Oil",
      "category": "Automotive"
    }
  ]
}
```

No Markdown.

No prose outside JSON.

Do not require Ollama to return the whole Category Manager.

=======================================================
STRICT RESPONSE VALIDATION
==========================

Treat Ollama output as untrusted.

Validate all results before modifying the workbook.

For every returned entry verify:

1. "excel_row" is an integer.

2. The row was actually requested.

3. The returned Sub-Category exactly corresponds to the expected target row, allowing only the same normalization conventions already used by ShopGraph where appropriate.

4. "category" is a non-empty string.

5. No target row occurs twice.

6. Ollama did not return a non-target row.

7. Every requested row appears exactly once.

8. Existing non-NA Category rows are never returned as targets.

9. The Category Manager row still has blank/NA Category immediately before write.

10. The Sub-Category identity has not changed since the Ollama request was created.

Reject malformed or incomplete batches instead of partially trusting them.

=======================================================
CATEGORY REUSE
==============

Build a case-insensitive lookup of existing Category names.

If Ollama returns an existing Category with only capitalization differences, normalize the result back to the EXACT spelling/capitalization already used in Category Manager.

Example:

Existing:

```
Household
```

Ollama:

```
household
```

Write:

```
Household
```

This prevents accidental duplicate broad Categories.

Do not automatically replace genuinely different new Category names.

=======================================================
NEW CATEGORY SAFETY
===================

Ollama is allowed to propose a genuinely new broad Category because the existing ShopGraph prompt already permits this.

However:

* reject blank values;
* reject NA as writable output;
* trim whitespace;
* reject obviously malformed values;
* do not allow multiline Category values;
* do not allow excessively long Category names;
* do not allow Ollama to modify Sub-Category or Products.

Use conservative validation without changing the intended taxonomy behavior.

=======================================================
PREVIEW BEFORE WRITE
====================

Before modifying the workbook, print the proposed changes.

For example:

```
Proposed Category assignments:

Row 17 | Fresh Fruit -> Food
Row 29 | Motor Oil -> Automotive
Row 41 | Baby Formula -> Baby
```

Also distinguish deterministic reuse from Ollama classification when practical.

Then continue with the validated write.

A confirmation prompt is not required unless existing ShopGraph conventions strongly favor one.

=======================================================
